SWEET Bluetooth: injection-position sweep (patch 0221, ticket T-2ed7, W-BTUP; boot-arg sweetbtleprobe=4, default behaviour unchanged, =1/2/3 unchanged). bt-up7 [obs 20261107T043123Z-bt-up7]: bluetoothd's 48 commands back to back leave 0x2007/0x200b/0x200c refused by the ROM (0x0c, sticky); a 0x2007 after EVERY step keeps them open but makes 0x204a (position 19) refuse. Question: does ONE 0x2007 after step k keep all of them open and 0x204a at 0x00, and for which k? The sweep runs 25 power-cycled passes of the byte-exact 48-command sequence of patch 0219 (k in 0,1,2,3,5,7,9,11,13,15,17,18,19,20,22,25,30,36,37,38,39,43,45,47,48; k=0 = no injection, the control that must reproduce bt-up7 pass A), each with ONE read-only 0x2007 after step k, then the final 0x2007, 0x200b (bluetoothd's random scan parameters) and 0x200c scan-enable OFF as in 0219 pass A, and prints one row per pass: `BT PROBE: sweep inj=K inj2007=.. 204a=.. final2007=.. 200b=.. 200c=.. diverged=N ... verdict=OPEN-ALL|OPEN-BUT-DIVERGED|REFUSED` plus a done row listing the OPEN-ALL positions. Same opcodes as 0219 (all sent by bluetoothd byte-exact), nothing that enables scan or advertising, no patchram, no flash, no baud change; volatile controller RAM state, cleared by the REG_ON power-cycle between the passes and after the sweep (as 0216/0217/0219). The per-transfer HCI byte trace is suppressed during the sweep (counted in the done row) so the 3000-transfer cap is left for bluetoothd. Applies after 0189, 0213, 0215, 0216, 0217 and 0219. Not run on hardware yet.

